The model files, and what each was made from
--------------------------------------------

The models are not in the repository. The package fetches them, and they are published with a
copy of this file.

  base.lsw              the shared speech model       LibriTTS-R, from an LJ Speech start
  hifi/vox.lsw          hifi's route to sound         LibriTTS-R and VocalSet
  fast/melvoc.lsw       fast's route for speech       decoder: LibriTTS-R, from the speech model;
                                                      Vocos: unmodified weights, MIT (below)
  fast/vocoder.lsw      fast's route for singing      LibriTTS-R, from an LJ Speech start
  voices/*.lsw          the sixteen voices, and       LibriTTS-R
  hifi/voices/*.lsw     each one's part for hifi
  fast/voices/*.lsw     and for fast
  breaks.lsw            pause model                   LibriTTS-R, from the speech model; taught in
                                                      part by BERT
  g2p.lsw               letter-to-sound model         CMU Pronouncing Dictionary
  lexicon.lsw           pronunciation lexicon         CMU Pronouncing Dictionary, plus Lip
                                                      Service's own entries
  motion/straight.lsw,  sung pitch motion             measured from VocalSet
  motion/vibrato.lsw,
  motion/wide.lsw

hifi/vox.lsw (sha256 cb78b1bd1317a9a2...) was trained on read speech from LibriTTS-R and
singing from VocalSet.

The engine's rule for an emphasized word was measured on LibriTTS-R's readers with the
Crowdsourced LibriTTS Speech Prominence Annotations (Max Morrison, 2023,
doi:10.5281/zenodo.10402793), used under CC BY 4.0. The annotations themselves are not
distributed.

The website's choir also plays recorded breaths: short excerpts of VocalSet recordings (CC BY
4.0, below), served by the site in breaths.bin. They are not in the repository or the package.


LibriTTS-R
----------
The speech model, the voices, both routes to sound and the pause model were trained on
LibriTTS-R.

  Yuma Koizumi, Heiga Zen, Shigeki Karita, Yifan Ding, Kohei Yatabe, Nobuyuki Morioka,
  Michiel Bacchiani, Yu Zhang, Wei Han, and Ankur Bapna. "LibriTTS-R: A Restored Multi-Speaker
  Text-to-Speech Corpus." Interspeech 2023. https://www.openslr.org/141/

  The LibriTTS-R corpus is made available by Google LLC under a Creative Commons Attribution
  4.0 International License: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

LibriTTS-R is derived from LibriTTS (Zen et al., Interspeech 2019), which is derived from
LibriSpeech (Panayotov et al., ICASSP 2015), which is drawn from LibriVox audiobook recordings
that their readers released into the public domain. The model files are models trained on the
recordings; no LibriTTS-R recording is distributed.

Each voice has an invented first name. No reader made, endorses or is associated with Lip
Service.

Each voice is tuned to one reader of LibriTTS-R, and the package's `voices` command prints that
reader's number in the corpus.


LJ Speech
---------
The speech model and the vocoder started from models trained on the LJ Speech Dataset; the mel
decoder and the pause model were trained from the speech model.

  Keith Ito and Linda Johnson. "The LJ Speech Dataset." 2017.
  https://keithito.com/LJ-Speech-Dataset/

  The LJ Speech Dataset is in the public domain.


VocalSet
--------
The sung pitch motions (motion/straight.lsw, motion/vibrato.lsw, motion/wide.lsw) were measured
from VocalSet's recordings, and hifi's route to sound (hifi/vox.lsw) was trained on them beside
LibriTTS-R. No VocalSet recording is in the model files.

The website's choir breaths (breaths.bin, served by the site only) are 76 short excerpts of
VocalSet recordings: the breaths singers took between notes. breaths.json, served beside it,
names the recording and the time span each one came from. Changes made: each was cut to the
breath alone, resampled to 24 kHz, set to a common level and raised (5.6 times that level), and
stored as 16-bit samples. The excerpts remain under VocalSet's license.

  Julia Wilkins, Prem Seetharaman, Alison Wahl, and Bryan Pardo. "VocalSet: A Singing Voice
  Dataset." ISMIR 2018. Version 1.2. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1442513

  VocalSet is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License:
  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/


Vocos
-----
fast/melvoc.lsw contains the weights of Vocos (charactr/vocos-mel-24khz), unchanged except that
its pointwise layers are stored as 8-bit integers. Vocos was trained by its authors on LibriTTS.
The engine's Vocos code is Lip Service's own implementation. These weights remain under the MIT
License below.

  Hubert Siuzdak. "Vocos: Closing the gap between time-domain and Fourier-based neural vocoders
  for high-quality audio synthesis." arXiv:2306.00814, 2023. https://github.com/gemelo-ai/vocos

